I only read things and changed nothing. The worktree for stream 1180 is gone: its cleanup status is "removed" (removed at 2026-11-16T09:12:44Z). I did not recreate it. I read the test from main on GitHub instead (apps/core/src/services/slots/host-budget.test.ts):

  test('host budget allows two claims', async () => {
    const started = Date.now()
    await claimBoth()
    expect(Date.now() - started).toBeLessThan(50)
  })

Likely cause: the test checks a wall-clock time limit. It passes only if claimBoth() finishes in under 50 ms, measured with Date.now(). That is a performance check, not a check of behaviour. On a shared, busy machine (CI or the squad box), normal delays like garbage collection, a busy event loop, slow first-time module or database setup, or CPU contention will sometimes go over 50 ms. That matches failing about one run in ten. Date.now() also only measures whole milliseconds and can jump if the system clock changes, which makes it worse.

There is a second problem: the name says "allows two claims", but the test never checks that both claims succeeded. It only checks that the call was fast.

Not verified: I couldn't find where claimBoth is defined from here, so I haven't ruled out extra causes inside it, such as real timers, retry or backoff sleeps, or state shared with other tests.

Suggested fix: remove the timing check and assert on the outcome instead (both claims granted, the budget used up as expected). If the goal is "claims don't wait", use fake timers, or check that no wait or backoff path ran.

