  })

Most likely cause: the test asserts a wall-clock time limit. It passes only if claimBoth() finishes within 50 ms of real time. That threshold has nothing to do with whether the host budget is correct. It depends on how busy the machine is. On a shared box with few CPUs, or on CI runners, a garbage-collection pause, scheduler delay, a cold import or JIT warm-up, or other tests running in parallel can push one run past 50 ms. That fits a failure rate of about one run in ten. Date.now() has millisecond resolution and can jump with clock changes, which adds a little more noise. The test's name says it checks that two claims are allowed, but the assertion checks speed, not that outcome.

Not confirmed: I could not see the claimBoth() helper because it is not in the snippet I had. If it does real I/O (database, Redis, timers, or retry or backoff), it adds delay that changes from run to run and makes this worse. I also did not run the test repeatedly, so this comes from reading the code, not from reproducing the failure.

Suggested fix for a follow-up stream: assert the result instead of the elapsed time (both claims granted, budget count correct). If speed matters, check it separately: either confirm with fake timers that claimBoth makes no timer or backoff waits, or use a much looser bound that is not part of the main test.
